Preservation lands are identified to protect undeveloped lands which form an important part of the region’s open space fabric but that are not valued primarily for agricultural uses. Such lands include important native (i.e., indigenous and endemic) Hawaiian plant, invertebrate and wildlife habitat, archaeological or historic sites, significant landforms or landscapes over which significant views are visible, recreational areas, agricultural areas, areas important to the health of the watershed, and areas hazardous to potential development.

Preservation lands generally include undeveloped lands that:

